Z||000000||Capt Fiero||08-04-2001||07:45 PM||||I tried my best to show the new wiring that you get with the adapter kit, instead of having 2 wires plugged into the coil you have one. As the harness takes both connectors and only gives you one to the coil and one to the MSD Box. I got a chance to really open it up last night, and I am really really impressed with how the MSD works at freeway speeds. Before it was always a bit more slugish than I liked in 3rd between 5000-6500 and now it pulls realyl strong.   <A HREF="http://photos.onebigvillage.com/pubgallery.asp?id_=2566" TARGET=_blank>http://photos.onebigvillage.com/pubgallery.asp?id_=2566</A>  <BR><P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://photos.onebigvillage.com/pubimage.asp?id_=32415"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fiero_Freak||08-05-2001||10:09 AM||||Ahhhh look at that smoke.  Must be a stick!  Anyway.  Does MSD offer an adapter kit for there ignition?  I had looked in summit and they had a crane fireball system that I was looking at because it had the wiring adapter.  If you have a part number or something I would appreciate it.  Thanks<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||California Kid||08-05-2001||12:32 PM||||Capt.....Just wanted to let you know that you have yours mounted in the exact same location that I did. Mine failed after 2 years use, because I feel it was acting like a "heat sink" mounted on the forward truck panel (not far from the header bank). Although I'm running a V8 with header a little closer(also have heat insulation at that location), I don't think it make too much difference.<P>Suggest that after you do some city driving, pop the truck and feel how warm the box is. Found that mine (disconnected but still mounted)was very hot to the touch.<P>Failure mode on mine was "Thermal", car would run fine for about 30 min. then shut down, wouldn't start for another 15 to 20 minutes. Took me awhile to determine it was MSD unit. Just thought I'd let you know.||||reg||4
Z||000003||gargoyle||08-05-2001||03:34 PM||||Are the wires on the module long enough so it could be mounted on the shelf on the left side or could the wires be extended? If this was possible it would remove the heat sinking effect.||||reg||3
Z||000004||California Kid||08-05-2001||05:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gargoyle:<BR><B>Are the wires on the module long enough so it could be mounted on the shelf on the left side or could the wires be extended? If this was possible it would remove the heat sinking effect.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Pretty sure some of the wires would have to be extended, no problem, just make sure to solder splices.<P>Haven't installed my new 6AL unit yet, still playing with location ideas. Checked out neat part from West Coast Fieros that relocates battery to front trunk, which would free up a lot of room in engine compartment, as another possible location away from headers.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Capt Fiero||08-05-2001||05:08 PM||||Yes there is an adapter kit, did you look at the pics that are up on the page, I even went out of my way to show you pics of that specific harness. MSD PN#8876||||reg||1
Z||000006||Capt Fiero||08-05-2001||05:27 PM||||This is a pic of the harness adapter, it plugs into the stock dual GM Coil Connector then has wires that go to the MSD Box and one connecter that plugs into the coil. (the other spot on the coil is no longer used.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://photos.onebigvillage.com/pubimage.asp?id_=35673">  <p>[This message has been edited by Capt Fiero (edited 08-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fiero_Freak||08-05-2001||05:58 PM||||Thanks for the part number.  I didn't know if the harness was MSD or something else.  I saw you had one, I just wanted to know who made it.  Thanks again... I'm calling summit right now.||||reg||1
